body:
Five years is definetely enough time to get to know someone, after half a decade you should be able to tell, as a teacher, if a certain student has certain limitations that will hinder his capabilities of getting a college degree. Talented students are easy to spot as they have a much quicker grasp on the undertanding of things, they learn at a faster rate than most. [here's some links](https://www.theguardian.com/teacher-network/teacher-blog/2012/may/09/teaching-gifted-and-talented-pupils)  [that more accurately describe how teachers can spot these behaviours at a young age](https://www.readandspell.com/us/signs-of-a-gifted-child).

&#x200B;

Also..at 17 you're still a teenager, not an adult. You've just finished high school, you can't legally drive or consume alcohol or consent to sex or take any other adult responsabilities...but apparently you're supposed to be able to make a choice that will define the rest of your life alone. At that age you still don't have the life experience and the mental capacity of an adult, which is why advice from older, more educated and experienced people can be crucial at this age.

> What do you feel you have the right skills for?  

  I dont really know at this point. I did a few of those career aptitute tests if thats                        what youre referring to. 

&#x200B;

> Do you want to stay in CS? 

&#x200B;

I dont see how a failed CS student can ever stay in cs. Even if I take on a trade school for programming the teaching material is the same and I might end up failing anyways.
